# The SELF-HOST reject pin for `directive scheduler=<unknown mode>`, wired as
# `sh_rejects_unknown_scheduler_mode` in tests/integration.rs.
#
# GRADUATED out of `known_gaps/` (was `sh_unknown_scheduler_mode_overaccepted.gg`,
# `#[ignore]`d, asserting the intended reject while the self-host over-accepted).
# It is here rather than merged into `unknown_scheduler_mode_error.gg` because it
# asserts something that fixture cannot: an ASYNC program whose scheduler choice
# is observable, so the pin fails loudly if a future change starts accepting the
# mode again and silently picks a runtime.
#
# Rust gg REJECTS this source with `error[E_UnknownDirective]`
# (`src/semantic/mod.rs`). The self-host now refuses it too, in
# `validate_directives` (`self_host_typechecker/resolve.gg`, twinned in
# `self_host_resolver/resolve.gg`).
#
# WHAT IT USED TO DO, MEASURED — this is why the reject is not cosmetic. The
# self-host performed NO directive validation at all, and
# `self_host_lowerer/lower.gg`'s scheduler arm has no `else`, so the value was
# discarded and the module kept `new_lir_module`'s POOL default. Emit + cc + run
# of THIS program, rc 0 at every stage, against the same program with
# `scheduler=single`:
#   scheduler=bogus   -> `// ── Scheduler: Pool (M:N thread pool …) ──`
#                        worker runs | after sleep | 1 | 1
#   scheduler=single  -> `// ── Scheduler: Single (N:1 cooperative loop) ──`
#                        after sleep | 0 | worker runs | 1
# A typo in the mode silently changed an async program's interleaving — Core #10
# lower-or-reject in its exact forbidden shape, on the lane the succession plan
# makes the reference.
#
# ⚠ THE PARITY GATE CANNOT SEE THIS CLASS. `self_host_runtime_diff` classifies a
# Rust-rejected program as RustRejected and returns BEFORE the self-host is
# invoked, so a self-host over-acceptance of a Rust-rejected program is
# structurally invisible to it (filed as `todo/t0870`). That is why the pin is an
# explicit self-host assertion and not a parity row.
#
# ⚠ The `sleep` here is deliberate: it is what makes the two schedulers
# distinguishable on stdout. That `sleep` is not a scheduling point under
# `single` is a SEPARATE filed gap (todo/t0822) and does not affect this one —
# either way the two modes differ, which is the property under test.

directive scheduler=bogus

from std.time import sleep

async void worker(int &counter):
    print("worker runs")
    counter = counter + 1

async void main():
    shared int x = 0
    Task[void] t = spawn worker(&x)
    sleep(0.1)
    print("after sleep")
    with x:
        print(x)
    t.await()
    print(x)
